eaching the threshold significantly earlier/later.\n   * Score accumulation rate: $R_{\\text{score}} = \\frac{100}{t_{\\text{trigger}}}$.\n3. **Macro-Goal Switching Latency ($\\tau_{\\text{switch}}$ \u2014 Hierarchical Only):**\n   * Number of steps elapsed from $t_{\\text{trigger}}$ until the high-level manager switches from $g_1 (\\text{Harvest})$ to $g_2 (\\text{Evade})$ or $g_3 (\\text{Combat})$.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Statistical Analysis Plan\n\n### 6.1 Linear Mixed-Effects Model (LMM)\nTo account for repeated measures across procedural evaluation seeds and agent seeds, fit a Linear Mixed-Effects Model:\n$$\\Delta t_{\\text{post}, ijk} = \\beta_0 + \\beta_{\\text{arch}} \\cdot \\text{Arch}_i + \\beta_{\\text{trig}} \\cdot \\text{Trigger}_j + \\beta_{\\text{inter}} \\cdot (\\text{Arch}_i \\times \\text{Trigger}_j) + u_{\\text{agent}, i} + u_{\\text{env}, k} + \\epsilon_{ijk}$$\nWhere:\n* $\\text{Arch}_i \\in \\{0 (\\text{Non-Hierarchical}), 1 (\\text{Hierarchical})\\}$\n* $\\text{Trigger}_j \\in \\{0 (\\text{Suppressed}), 1 (\\text{Triggered})\\}$\n* $u_{\\text{agent}, i} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma_{\\text{agent}}^2)$ is the random effect of agent training seed.\n* $u_{\\text{env}, k} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma_{\\text{env}}^2)$ is the random effect of procedural environment test seed.\n* **Key Test:** The interaction coefficient $\\beta_{\\text{inter}}$. A statistically significant positive coefficient ($\\beta_{\\text{inter}} > 0$ with $p < 0.05$) demonstrates that hierarchical agents preserve survival duration significantly better when the mechanic triggers.\n\n### 6.2 Survival Analysis (Cox Proportional Hazards)\nFit a Cox Proportional Hazards regression model on post-trigger survival data:\n$$\\lambda(t | X) = \\lambda_0(t) \\exp\\left(\\beta_1 \\text{Arch} + \\beta_2 \\text{Trigger} + \\beta_3 (\\text{Arch} \\times \\text{Trigger}) + \\beta_4 t_{\\text{trigger}}\\right)$$\n* Compute Hazard Ratios (HR) with 95% confidence intervals.\n* Perform a two-sided Log-Rank test between survival distributions of Hierarchical vs. Non-Hierarchical agents in Condition $T_1$.\n\n### 6.3 Multiple Comparison Corrections\n* Family-wise error rate controlled at $\\alpha = 0.05$ using Bonferroni-Holm adjustment across all primary and secondary hypotheses.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Threats to Validity & Countermeasures\n\n| Threat to Validity | Potential Confound | Experimental Countermeasure |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Capacity Asymmetry** | HRL has multiple policy heads, potentially providing more capacity than the baseline. | Parameter budgets are strictly equalized ($\\approx 1.25\\text{M}$ parameters) by scaling down hidden dimensions of the manager/worker components. |\n| **Time-to-Trigger Confound** | An agent that reaches the score threshold faster might face a different state distribution or local clutter. | Include $t_{\\text{trigger}}$ as a continuous control covariate in the mixed-effects regression; confirm that mean $t_{\\text{trigger}}$ does not significantly differ between architectures via two-sample t-test. |\n| **Mechanic Avoidance (Reward Hacking)** | Agents might learn to deliberately avoid crossing score 100 to prevent the invasion. | Constant per-step penalty ($r_{\\text{step}} = -0.005$) and dense scoring incentives ensure that stalling yields strongly sub-optimal cumulative episode return. |\n| **Procedural Seed Variance** | Specific island or entity layouts could favor one architecture. | Paired evaluation design: each agent seed is tested on identical procedural environment seeds across both $T_0$ and $T_1$ conditions. |\n| **Right-Censoring at Horizon** | Agents surviving until $T_{\\max} = 2,500$ steps truncate survival metrics. | Formal survival analysis (Kaplan-Meier and Cox Proportional Hazards) handles right-censored data without artificial distortion of survival duration means. |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Summary of Experimental Execution Feasibility\n\n* **Reproducibility:** The environment logic relies purely on deterministic pseudo-random number generators seeded per episode.\n* **Compute Budget:** $10^7$ steps across 80 training seeds (40 primary + 40 ablation) is standard for discrete-action 2D environments, typically requiring $\\approx 150-200$ GPU-hours on standard compute infrastructure (e.g., NVIDIA A100 or RTX 4090).\n* **Interpretability:** By comparing identical checkpoints across both triggered ($T_1$) and suppressed ($T_0$) modes, the design cleanly isolates the specific causal effect of the pirate invasion trigger from general background environment progression.", "stage": "result"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "dvzTasm0HZegpucPzfLCsAQ", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 13}
